WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.080
Two people move the same total air each minute.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.640
So why is one of them building up carbon dioxide while the other is not?

3
00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:16.263
The first film ended at a dead-ended sac, the alveolus.

4
00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:19.863
Blood can at best match the gas inside it.

5
00:00:20.963 --> 00:00:22.603
Blood flow keeps bringing new blood.

6
00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:26.523
But the gas must be renewed too, through the airways.

7
00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:29.483
So what keeps moving fresh air in?

8
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:32.153
A mammal pulls air in.

9
00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:37.373
The diaphragm, a dome of muscle under the lungs, contracts and flattens.

10
00:00:38.553 --> 00:00:42.373
The chest enlarges, pressure in the lungs falls, and air flows in.

11
00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:44.593
This is aspiration.

12
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:47.627
But a tube leads to the sac.

13
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:53.627
The airways, from nose and mouth down to the smallest bronchioles with no alveoli,

14
00:00:54.127 --> 00:00:57.167
exchange nothing. They are dead space.

15
00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:02.637
So the first air into the sac each breath is stale air left in the tube.

16
00:01:03.937 --> 00:01:08.157
And fresh air still in the tube when you stop breathing in is breathed out unused.

17
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.890
Take slow, deep breaths.

18
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.590
Each breath clears the dead space and sends a large fresh portion into the sac.

19
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.770
The total air moved each minute is minute ventilation.

20
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:25.550
The fresh part reaching the alveoli is alveolar ventilation.

21
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.410
Now keep the total air per minute the same, but take smaller, faster breaths.

22
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.310
Does carbon dioxide in the sac rise, fall or stay the same?

23
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.600
It rises. Each small breath barely clears the dead space.

24
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.900
Less fresh air reaches the sac, while the body makes carbon dioxide as fast as

25
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:54.900
before.

26
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.030
Here the first film's rule runs in reverse.

27
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.850
Blood giving up carbon dioxide leaves with no lower partial pressure than the gas it

28
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.590
last touched. So the blood's carbon dioxide climbs with the sac's.

29
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:12.543
The brainstem watches that result.

30
00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:17.823
Carbon dioxide crosses into the brain's fluid and lowers its pH.

31
00:02:19.443 --> 00:02:23.923
Sensors near the brainstem surface detect the fall, and the drive to breathe rises.

32
00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:28.873
Haldane and Priestley sampled the last of a forced breath out.

33
00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:35.233
In resting subjects, a small rise in alveolar carbon dioxide greatly increased

34
00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:39.773
breathing. Lowering oxygen did little until it fell far.

35
00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:43.557
So breathing keeps the gas the blood touches fresh.

36
00:02:44.857 --> 00:02:48.497
At rest, a mammal's controller mostly reads carbon dioxide.

37
00:02:49.717 --> 00:02:54.957
That level is the result of its own breathing, set against the body's carbon dioxide

38
00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:59.577
output. Too little fresh air, and carbon dioxide climbs.

39
00:03:00.957 --> 00:03:03.177
The sensors then push breathing back up.
